<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections. Reading: Create simple questions based on reading materials. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond to texts about meal planning, with personal reflections (e.g., "I like cooking with my family."). Can create simple questions and infer answers from reading materials, using context (e.g., "What ingredients do we need for a healthy meal?" "We need some rice and vegetables."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes by recognizing and applying simple prefixes and suffixes to form new words (e.g., "-un" in "uncountable" and "-ing" in "cooking").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of various fruits (e.g., mangos, watermelon) and vegetables (e.g., tomatoes, lettuce). Students say the names of the food items as the teacher presents them. The teacher then asks, "Are fruits and vegetables healthy?" to activate prior knowledge about the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for 'lettuce' and a flashcard for 'fruit'. The teacher points to a sentence on the board: "Lettuce is a vegetable. Mangos are fruit." The teacher reads the sentences clearly, emphasizing the /v/ sound in "vegetables" and the /f/ sound in "fruit". Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models reading a short sentence related to daily meals: "I often eat rice for lunch."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is a tomato a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected answer: A vegetable.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Do we eat juice or drink juice?" (Expected answer: Drink juice.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: The teacher shows a picture of a full plate of food and asks, "Is this a meal or a snack?" (Expected answer: A meal.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated paragraph about a family's meal plan (Genre: simple narrative/description; Register: informal; Format: text on a worksheet or whiteboard). The text includes target vocabulary (e.g., breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, cook, eat, enjoy, healthy, often, sometimes, some, many) and grammatical structures (Present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns like 'some rice', 'many fruits').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b><br>
                "My family loves healthy food! For <b>breakfast</b>, we often eat <b>fresh fruits</b> like <b>mangos</b> and <b>watermelon</b>. We also drink some <b>juice</b> and <b>water</b>. For <b>lunch</b>, my mom <b>cooks</b> some <b>rice</b> and <b>beans</b>. We always add fresh <b>vegetables</b> like <b>tomatoes</b> and <b>lettuce</b>. Sometimes, we have a <b>snack</b> in the afternoon, maybe a banana. For <b>dinner</b>, we like to eat chicken and more <b>vegetables</b>. My family enjoys <b>cooking</b> and eating together. <b>Fruits</b> and <b>vegetables</b> are very <b>tasty</b> and good for us! We can eat many healthy meals."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student reads and understands simple, short texts related to daily routines and food preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks: "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: A family and their healthy meals.) "What do they eat a lot of?" (Expected answer: Fruits and vegetables.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read statements and mark them True (T) or False (F) based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The family eats pizza for breakfast. (F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. They like mangos and watermelon. (T)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Mom cooks rice and beans for dinner. (F)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information, identifying key vocabulary, understanding simple sentence structures (present simple, modal verbs 'can', countable/uncountable nouns). Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing and applying simple prefixes and suffixes (-un, -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Reading):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the main text where target vocabulary words are missing. They read each sentence and choose the correct word from a provided word bank to complete it. For example: "For breakfast, we often eat fresh ______." (Word bank: fruits, juice, beans). Teacher guides students to read the completed sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Vocabulary & Phonemic Awareness Reading:</b> The teacher points to key words in the text like "cooking", "eating", "vegetables", "fruits", "uncountable" (if introduced). Students read these words aloud, focusing on the suffixes "-ing" and the prefix "-un" (e.g., in "uncountable", clarifying its meaning by saying we cannot count individual rice grains). The teacher also reviews the pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit" by having students read these words from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Scanning for Information (Pair Reading):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of simple questions (e.g., "What do they drink for breakfast?", "What do they eat for lunch?"). They must read the main text silently to find the answers to their questions. After finding all answers, they take turns reading a question aloud to their partner, who then reads the answer directly from the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task using the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group activity (3-4 students). Each group receives a new, short paragraph describing a different family's meal plan (similar to the Stage 2 text, using the same vocabulary and grammar at A1.3 level). The text explicitly mentions "healthy" or "unhealthy" items where appropriate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students read their group's new meal plan text silently to understand it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Each group then works collaboratively to create 2-3 simple questions based *only* on their text (e.g., "What do they cook for dinner?", "Do they eat many fruits?"). They write these questions on a separate sheet of paper. This directly addresses "Create simple questions based on reading materials."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Groups exchange their written questions with another group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The new group reads the received questions and then *scans* the original text (from the first group) to find and infer the answers. They write down the answers next to each question.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Finally, each student reads one meal idea from the text and provides a personal reflection: "I like/don't like [food/meal idea] from this plan because it is [tasty/healthy/sweet/salty]." This fulfills "Respond to texts with personal reflections."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students are actively reading about and interacting with information related to "Planning Meals for My Family" and recognizing "Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4, noting students' ability to create questions based on a text, find answers in a text, and use target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the groups' written questions and answers from Stage 4. Additionally, students individually complete a short reading comprehension quiz: a simple paragraph with 2-3 multiple-choice questions or fill-in-the-blanks using target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to create clear and simple questions based on the provided text (e.g., "What ingredients do we need for a healthy meal?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in locating and inferring answers from the reading material (e.g., "We need some rice and vegetables.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, quantifiers) in questions and answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of decoding basic word parts (e.g., recognizing '-ing' in 'cooking').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of personal reflections based on reading.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection sheet.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand short texts about family meal plans." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can create questions and find answers in a text." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about healthy food did you read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to find the answers in the texts? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading strategies worked best for 5th graders (e.g., scanning, T/F, picture support)? Were students able to create appropriate questions and respond with personal reflections? Was the level of the reading text appropriate for A1.3? What adjustments are needed for reinforcing phonemic awareness and reading comprehension for the next lesson? Which vocabulary items need further review?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided students with knowledge and vocabulary about meal planning. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use this information to actively discuss their own food preferences and meal ideas, applying the vocabulary and grammatical structures from the texts in communicative dialogues and peer interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[3-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>